    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p80-q47-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that $\\dfrac{a}{h}+k=6$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x(0)=\\frac{a}{h}+k=6$.",
          "worked": "Initially $t=0$ and $x=6$. \n\n$$\\begin{align*}x(0)&=\\frac{a}{0+h}+k\\\\6&=\\frac{a}{h}+k.\\end{align*}$$\n\n",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 22
      },
      {
        "id": "p80-q47-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "After one second, the particle is two metres to the left of its original position. Show that $a=2h(h+1)$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x(1)=4$; subtracting $x(1)$ from $x(0)$ gives $\\frac{a}{[h(h+1)]}=2$, hence $a=2h(h+1)$.",
          "worked": "Two metres left of $x=6$ is $x=4$, so $x(1)=4$. Subtract this from the initial-position equation. \n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{a}{h+1}+k&=4\\\\\\frac{a}{h}-\\frac{a}{h+1}&=6-4\\\\\\frac{a}{h(h+1)}&=2\\\\a&=2h(h+1).\\end{align*}$$\n\n",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 48
      },
      {
        "id": "p80-q47-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Show that $a=4h^2$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x'(t)=-\\frac{a}{(t+h)^2}$; leftward speed $4$ means $x'(0)=-4$, so $a=4h^2$.",
          "worked": "A leftward speed of $4\\,\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$ means initial velocity $-4\\,\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$. \n\n$$\\begin{align*}x'(t)&=-\\frac{a}{(t+h)^2}\\\\x'(0)&=-\\frac{a}{h^2}\\\\-4&=-\\frac{a}{h^2}\\\\a&=4h^2.\\end{align*}$$\n\n",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 48
      },
      {
        "id": "p80-q47-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "Find the displacement-time equation $x(t)$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p80-q47-d-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "x(t)=\\frac{4}{t+1}+2",
                    "display": false
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p80-q47-d-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Equate the results from (b) and (c) to get ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "h=1",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ", then use (a) to find ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "a=4,\\ k=2",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "Equate the expressions for $a$. Since $x(0)$ is defined, $h\\ne0$. \n\n$$\\begin{align*}2h(h+1)&=4h^2\\\\h+1&=2h\\\\h&=1\\\\a&=4h^2=4\\\\k&=6-\\frac{a}{h}=2\\\\x(t)&=\\frac{4}{t+1}+2.\\end{align*}$$\n\n",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 54
      }
    ]
